ge R, Jung D, Lejarraga I, de la Paz D, Cordero JM (2022) Assessment of the Madrid region air quality zoning based on mesoscale modelling and k-means clustering. Atmos Environ 287:119258</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="doi">10.1016/j.atmosenv.2022.119258</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Wang J, Wu T, Mao J, Chen H (2024) A forecasting framework on fusion of spatiotemporal features for multi-station PM2.5. Expert Syst Appl 238:121951</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="doi">10.1016/j.eswa.2023.121951</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference></ReferenceList></PubmedData></PubmedArticle><PubmedArticle><MedlineCitation Status="MEDLINE" Owner="NLM" IndexingMethod="Automated"><PMID Version="1">40165707</PMID><DateCompleted><Year>2025</Year><Month>05</Month><Day>15</Day></DateCompleted><DateRevised><Year>2026</Year><Month>09</Month><Day>24</Day></DateRevised><Article PubModel="Print"><Journal><ISSN IssnType="Electronic">1523-1739</ISSN><JournalIssue CitedMedium="Internet"><Volume>39</Volume><Issue>2</Issue><PubDate><Year>2025</Year><Month>Apr</Month></PubDate></JournalIssue><Title>Conservation biology : the journal of the Society for Conservation Biology</Title><ISOAbbreviation>Conserv Biol</ISOAbbreviation></Journal><ArticleTitle>New opportunities and challenges for conservation evidence synthesis from advances in natural language processing.</ArticleTitle><Pagination><StartPage>e14464</StartPage><MedlinePgn>e14464</MedlinePgn></Pagination><ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">e14464</ELocationID><ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1111/cobi.14464</ELocationID><Abstract><AbstractText>Addressing global environmental conservation problems requires rapidly translating natural and conservation social science evidence to policy-relevant information. Yet, exponential increases in scientific production combined with disciplinary differences in reporting research make interdisciplinary evidence syntheses especially challenging. Ongoing developments in natural language processing (NLP), such as large language models, machine learning (ML), and data mining, hold the promise of accelerating cross-disciplinary evidence syntheses and primary research. The evolution of ML, NLP, and artificial intelligence (AI) systems in computational science research provides new approaches to accelerate all stages of evidence synthesis in conservation social science. To show how ML, language processing, and AI can help automate and scale evidence syntheses in conservation social science, we describe methods that can automate querying the literature, process large and unstructured bodies of textual evidence, and extract parameters of interest from scientific studies. Automation can translate to other research agendas in conservation social science by categorizing and labeling data at scale, yet there are major unanswered questions about how to use hybrid AI-expert systems ethically and effectively in conservation.</AbstractText><CopyrightInformation>&#xa9; 2025 The Author(s). Conservation Biology published by Wiley Periodicals LLC on behalf of Society for Conservation Biology.</CopyrightInformation></Abstract><AuthorList CompleteYN="Y"><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Chang</LastName><ForeName>Charlotte H</ForeName><Initials>CH</Initials><Identifier Source="ORCID">0000-0002-1274-7721</Identifier><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Department of Biology and Environmental Analysis Program, Pomona College, Claremont, California, USA.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Cook-Patton</LastName><ForeName>Susan C</ForeName><Initials>SC</Initials><Identifier Source="ORCID">0000-0002-7194-4397</Identifier><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>The Nature Conservancy, Arlington, Virginia, USA.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Erbaugh</LastName><ForeName>James T</ForeName><Initials>JT</Initials><Identifier Source="ORCID">0000-0002-0602-9045</Identifier><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Department of Environmental Studies, Dartmouth College, Hanover, New Hampshire, USA.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Lu</LastName><ForeName>Luci</ForeName><Initials>L</Initials><Identifier Source="ORCID">0000-0003-2943-4032</Identifier><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Jornada Experimental Range, New Mexico State University, Las Cruces, New Mexico, USA.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Masuda</LastName><ForeName>Yuta J</ForeName><Initials>YJ</Initials><Identifier Source="ORCID">0000-0002-1698-4855</Identifier><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Paul G. Allen Family Foundation, Seattle, Washington, USA.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Moln&#xe1;r</LastName><ForeName>Istv&#xe1;n</ForeName><Initials>I</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Lexunit Zrt, Budapest, Hungary.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Papp</LastName><ForeName>D&#xe1;vid</ForeName><Initials>D</Initials><Identifier Source="ORCID">0000-0002-8814-2745</Identifier><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Lexunit Zrt, Budapest, Hungary.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Department of Telecommunications and Media Informatics, Budapest University of Technology and Economics, Budapest, Hungary.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Robinson</LastName><ForeName>Brian E</ForeName><Initials>BE</Initials><Identifier Source="ORCID">0000-0002-8972-8318</Identifier><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Department of Geography, McGill University, Montreal, Quebec, Canada.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author></AuthorList><Language>eng</Language><GrantList CompleteYN="Y"><Grant><Agency>Nature Conservancy</Agency><Country/></Grant><Grant><Agency>Bezos Earth Fund</Agency><Country/></Grant></GrantList><PublicationTypeList><PublicationType UI="D016428">Journal Article</PublicationType><PublicationType UI="D013485">Research Support, Non-U.S. Gov't</PublicationType></PublicationTypeList></Article><MedlineJournalInfo><Country>United States</Country><MedlineTA>Conserv Biol</MedlineTA><NlmUniqueID>9882301</NlmUniqueID><ISSNLinking>0888-8892</ISSNLinking></MedlineJournalInfo><CitationSubset>IM</CitationSubset><MeshHeadingList><MeshHeading><DescriptorName UI="D009323" MajorTopicYN="Y">Natural Language Processing</DescriptorName></MeshHeading><MeshHeading><DescriptorName UI="D003247" MajorTopicYN="Y">Conservation of Natural Resources</DescriptorName><QualifierName UI="Q000379" MajorTopicYN="N">methods</QualifierName></MeshHeading><MeshHeading><DescriptorName UI="D001185" MajorTopicYN="N">Artificial Intelligence</DescriptorName></MeshHeading><MeshHeading><DescriptorName UI="D000069550" MajorTopicYN="N">Machine Learning</DescriptorName></MeshHeading><MeshHeading><DescriptorName UI="D057225" MajorTopicYN="Y">Data Mining</DescriptorName></MeshHeading><MeshHeading><DescriptorName UI="D012942" MajorTopicYN="Y">Social Sciences</DescriptorName><QualifierName UI="Q000379" MajorTopicYN="N">methods</QualifierName></MeshHeading></MeshHeadingList><OtherAbstract Type="Publisher" Language="spa"><AbstractText>Nuevas oportunidades y retos para la s&#xed;ntesis de evidencias de la conservaci&#xf3;n a partir de los avances en el procesamiento ling&#xfc;&#xed;stico natural Resumen Abordar los problemas mundiales de conservaci&#xf3;n del medio ambiente exige traducir r&#xe1;pidamente los datos de las ciencias naturales y sociales de la conservaci&#xf3;n en informaci&#xf3;n relevante para las pol&#xed;ticas. Sin embargo, el aumento exponencial de la producci&#xf3;n cient&#xed;fica, combinado con las diferencias disciplinarias al momento de informar sobre la investigaci&#xf3;n, dificulta importantemente la s&#xed;ntesis interdisciplinaria de datos. Los avances actuales en el procesamiento ling&#xfc;&#xed;stico natural, como los grandes modelos ling&#xfc;&#xed;sticos, el aprendizaje autom&#xe1;tico y la miner&#xed;a de datos, prometen acelerar la s&#xed;ntesis interdisciplinaria de datos y la investigaci&#xf3;n primaria. La evoluci&#xf3;n de los sistemas de aprendizaje autom&#xe1;tico, procesamiento ling&#xfc;&#xed;stico natural e inteligencia artificial en la investigaci&#xf3;n cient&#xed;fica computacional ofrecen nuevos enfoques para acelerar todas las etapas de la s&#xed;ntesis de evidencias en las ciencias sociales de la conservaci&#xf3;n. Para mostrar c&#xf3;mo el aprendizaje autom&#xe1;tico, el procesamiento del lenguaje y la IA pueden ayudar a automatizar y ampliar las s&#xed;ntesis de pruebas en las ciencias sociales de la conservaci&#xf3;n, describimos m&#xe9;todos que pueden automatizar la consulta de la bibliograf&#xed;a, el procesamiento de grandes vol&#xfa;menes de pruebas textuales no estructuradas y la extracci&#xf3;n de par&#xe1;metros de inter&#xe9;s de los estudios cient&#xed;ficos. La automatizaci&#xf3;n puede aplicarse a otros programas de investigaci&#xf3;n en las ciencias sociales de la conservaci&#xf3;n mediante la categorizaci&#xf3;n y el etiquetado de datos a gran escala, pero a&#xfa;n quedan muchas preguntas por responder sobre c&#xf3;mo utilizar sistemas h&#xed;bridos de IA y expertos de forma &#xe9;tica y eficaz en la conservaci&#xf3;n.</AbstractText><CopyrightInformation>&#xa9; 2025 The Author(s). Conservation Biology published by Wiley Periodicals LLC on behalf of Society for Conservation Biology.</CopyrightInformation></OtherAbstract><KeywordList Owner="NOTNLM"><Keyword MajorTopicYN="N">aprendizaje autom&#xe1;tico</Keyword><Keyword MajorTopicYN="N">ciencias sociales de la conservaci&#xf3;n</Keyword><Keyword MajorTopicYN="N">conservation social science</Keyword><Keyword MajorTopicYN="N">evidence synthesis</Keyword><Keyword MajorTopicYN="N">language models</Keyword><Keyword MajorTopicYN="N">machine learning</Keyword><Keyword MajorTopicYN="N">modelos ling&#xfc;&#xed;sticos</Keyword><Keyword MajorTopicYN="N">natural language processing</Keyword><Keyword M